  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The relation between the stopping potential and the frequency of the incident light is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Substitute the value of the frequency corresponding to zero stopping potential into equation (2) to calculate the required work function of the material.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#981;</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>626</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#183;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>14</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>eV</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>626</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>07</mn><mi>eV</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p></blockquote>",
